# Gate-critical text must stay LF: sha digest of shipped migrations and bash parsers.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
AGENTS.md text eol=lf
docs/gate.md text eol=lf
*.mdc text eol=lf
# The CRAP ratchet reads this with awk '$1 == k {print $2}' and then compares
# arithmetically. With CRLF the key still matches but the number carries the
# CR, (( cc > base )) dies on a syntax error, and the elif is taken as false —
# the ratchet stops guarding and says nothing. core.autocrlf hands a fresh
# clone CRLF unless this line is here.
scripts/lizard-baseline.txt text eol=lf

# Everything below is read back from disk by the crate or by its contracts and
# compared by content. Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, so
# without these lines a fresh clone holds them in CRLF, and a needle with a line
# break stops matching: the SIL of 0.27 was red on a new Windows clone and
# green on the tree that published it. local_gate_contract.rs checks out every
# one of them with core.autocrlf=true and wants LF back:
# a_fresh_clone_with_autocrlf_checks_out_the_text_the_crate_reads_back_in_lf
#
# The crate's own source: tests read it with include_str! and look for code
# spanning lines. rustc gives a literal a bare LF whatever the file holds, and
# include_str! gives the bytes on disk.
*.rs text eol=lf
# The panel is embedded with include_str!, so without this the binary served
# different bytes depending on who checked it out.
*.html text eol=lf
# Docs, manifests, workflows and scripts the contracts read: doc_contract,
# ci_contract, cli_contract, local_gate_contract, e2e_tool_matrix_contract.
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
# The eval datasets the tests load, one JSON object per line.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
# Packaging goldens: packaging_contract compares them with what service.rs
# renders, which is LF, and they are copied to Linux hosts where systemd reads a
# trailing CR as part of the value (an ExecStart path, an environment variable).
*.service text eol=lf
*.socket text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
# Deliberately absent: packaging/windows/memory-industry.cmd. cmd.exe is its
# only reader and runs it in either ending, since it has no label or goto (what
# an LF-only batch file breaks); the binary itself writes it in LF.
# packaging_contract compares it after normalising \r\n, so no rule here would
# change a verdict, and pinning one would decide something nobody measured.

# `schtasks /Create /XML` reads the bytes, not the declaration: a UTF-8 task
# file is refused at (1,40) — the column `encoding=` sits in — before it is ever
# parsed. So the working tree has to hold UTF-16LE with a BOM, which is what
# makes this file one an operator hands to the scheduler straight out of the
# clone instead of converting first. `working-tree-encoding` keeps the blob git
# stores as UTF-8, so the diff is still text in review. Needs git 2.18+; JGit
# and libgit2 do not implement it, and a client without support would commit
# the UTF-16 bytes as-is.
packaging/windows/memory-industry-task.xml text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-16LE-BOM